1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,897
The night of November 27th, 1950.

2
00:00:02,917 --> 00:00:04,569
The Chosin Reservoir,

3
00:00:04,589 --> 00:00:07,687
high on North Korea's Kaema Plateau.

4
00:00:07,724 --> 00:00:09,731
Temperatures plunged below minus

5
00:00:09,751 --> 00:00:11,894
30 degrees Celsius, and even

6
00:00:11,914 --> 00:00:14,289
the oil in the rifles froze.

7
00:00:14,508 --> 00:00:16,122
Then, from every surrounding

8
00:00:16,142 --> 00:00:18,409
mountain, bugles sounded.

9
00:00:18,515 --> 00:00:22,422
The Chinese 9th Army Group, some 120,000 men,

10
00:00:22,442 --> 00:00:25,044
had encircled the 1st Marine Division.

11
00:00:25,281 --> 00:00:27,350
The Marines had only one way out:

12
00:00:27,479 --> 00:00:29,735
a single unpaved mountain road

13
00:00:29,755 --> 00:00:32,399
running about 126 kilometers

14
00:00:32,419 --> 00:00:34,756
through frozen gorges to the sea.

15
00:00:35,009 --> 00:00:37,532
About 30,000 UN troops,

16
00:00:37,552 --> 00:00:40,013
built around one Marine division,

17
00:00:40,033 --> 00:00:42,813
against four times as many Chinese.

18
00:00:42,948 --> 00:00:44,533
One of history's most brutal

19
00:00:44,553 --> 00:00:47,208
encirclements was about to begin.

20
00:00:47,578 --> 00:00:49,240
And yet two weeks later,

21
00:00:49,295 --> 00:00:51,699
the Marines reached the sea unbroken,

22
00:00:51,719 --> 00:00:53,225
bringing their equipment,

23
00:00:53,245 --> 00:00:55,910
their wounded, and even their dead.

24
00:00:56,156 --> 00:00:57,985
A withdrawal that its commander

25
00:00:58,005 --> 00:00:59,623
called not a retreat,

26
00:00:59,643 --> 00:01:02,023
but an attack in another direction.

27
00:01:02,043 --> 00:01:03,617
This is the miracle of the frozen

28
00:01:03,637 --> 00:01:06,842
gorge: the Battle of Chosin Reservoir.

29
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:10,209
In October 1950, after the Inchon landing,

30
00:01:10,229 --> 00:01:12,951
UN forces were driving north.

31
00:01:13,057 --> 00:01:14,483
Everyone believed the war

32
00:01:14,503 --> 00:01:16,466
would be over by Christmas.

33
00:01:16,709 --> 00:01:18,775
Under X Corps, the 1st Marine

34
00:01:18,795 --> 00:01:20,995
Division advanced north on the eastern

35
00:01:21,015 --> 00:01:23,239
front toward the Yalu River.

36
00:01:23,259 --> 00:01:24,742
Its objective lay deep in the

37
00:01:24,762 --> 00:01:27,110
Kaema Plateau, beyond the west

38
00:01:27,130 --> 00:01:29,350
side of the Chosin Reservoir.

39
00:01:29,598 --> 00:01:31,885
The division commander, Major General Oliver

40
00:01:31,905 --> 00:01:34,958
Smith, was uneasy about this advance.

41
00:01:35,113 --> 00:01:36,902
His units were being strung out far

42
00:01:36,922 --> 00:01:39,404
too thinly along a mountain road.

43
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:40,953
He had fought through the bloody

44
00:01:40,973 --> 00:01:42,786
island battles of Peleliu

45
00:01:42,806 --> 00:01:45,506
and Okinawa in the Pacific War.

46
00:01:45,616 --> 00:01:47,724
He knew better than anyone how dangerous a

47
00:01:47,744 --> 00:01:51,073
long column deep in enemy country could be.

48
00:01:51,301 --> 00:01:54,023
Smith deliberately slowed the advance.

49
00:01:54,153 --> 00:01:56,728
He stockpiled supplies at Hagaru-ri,

50
00:01:56,748 --> 00:01:59,380
built an airstrip, and left troops to

51
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,816
guard every key point on the road.

52
00:02:02,047 --> 00:02:04,133
Lay this road out by altitude,

53
00:02:04,153 --> 00:02:06,249
and you can see why it was a trap.

54
00:02:06,376 --> 00:02:08,633
It starts on a plateau over 1,000

55
00:02:08,653 --> 00:02:11,274
meters high, then drops almost straight

56
00:02:11,294 --> 00:02:13,852
to the sea past Funchilin Pass.

57
00:02:14,074 --> 00:02:16,137
Sheer mountains on both sides,

58
00:02:16,157 --> 00:02:19,187
and a road barely wide enough for one truck.

59
00:02:19,218 --> 00:02:20,848
Block a single pass, and the

60
00:02:20,868 --> 00:02:22,875
entire division would be trapped.

61
00:02:23,097 --> 00:02:24,420
And in those mountains,

62
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:26,900
the Chinese were already hiding.

63
00:02:27,019 --> 00:02:29,241
Song Shilun's 9th Army Group,

64
00:02:29,261 --> 00:02:32,109
twelve divisions strong, was waiting for

65
00:02:32,129 --> 00:02:34,311
the Marines to come deep enough.

66
00:02:34,523 --> 00:02:36,303
Signs that China had entered the

67
00:02:36,323 --> 00:02:38,761
war appeared as early as October.

68
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:41,066
But UN headquarters judged that no

69
00:02:41,086 --> 00:02:43,384
large-scale intervention would come.

70
00:02:43,624 --> 00:02:45,588
The night of November 27th.

71
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:48,063
Chinese troops swarmed the Marine positions

72
00:02:48,083 --> 00:02:50,153
at Yudam-ni from every direction.

73
00:02:50,374 --> 00:02:52,831
At the same time, the Chinese cut the

74
00:02:52,851 --> 00:02:56,024
road between Hagaru-ri and Koto-ri.

75
00:02:56,046 --> 00:02:58,052
In an instant, the Marines were

76
00:02:58,072 --> 00:03:00,241
chopped into isolated islands.

77
00:03:00,493 --> 00:03:02,566
Worse than the enemy was the cold.

78
00:03:02,656 --> 00:03:05,345
Canteens froze solid, and medics

79
00:03:05,365 --> 00:03:06,915
reportedly thawed frozen

80
00:03:06,935 --> 00:03:09,444
morphine syrettes in their mouths.

81
00:03:09,673 --> 00:03:11,986
By night came wave after wave of

82
00:03:12,006 --> 00:03:16,038
Chinese attacks; by day, endless cold.

83
00:03:16,093 --> 00:03:18,828
In thin cotton uniforms, the Chinese

84
00:03:18,848 --> 00:03:21,369
lost even more men to frostbite.

85
00:03:21,595 --> 00:03:24,333
The Chinese soldiers endured the same cold.

86
00:03:24,388 --> 00:03:27,977
Canvas shoes like sneakers, frozen rice balls.

87
00:03:27,997 --> 00:03:30,583
Many reportedly froze to death in their

88
00:03:30,603 --> 00:03:33,366
foxholes, still gripping their rifles.

89
00:03:33,596 --> 00:03:36,015
East of the reservoir, the U.S. Army's

90
00:03:36,035 --> 00:03:39,190
31st Regimental Combat Team was cut off.

91
00:03:39,271 --> 00:03:42,392
Of about 2,500 men, only around

92
00:03:42,412 --> 00:03:45,510
1,050 made it back to Hagaru-ri.

93
00:03:45,753 --> 00:03:47,263
And between Yudam-ni and

94
00:03:47,283 --> 00:03:49,812
Hagaru-ri lay Toktong Pass.

95
00:03:49,959 --> 00:03:53,786
Holding it, about 240 men of Fox Company held out,

96
00:03:53,806 --> 00:03:57,083
surrounded, for five days and nights.

97
00:03:57,310 --> 00:03:59,532
Wounded in the leg, company commander

98
00:03:59,552 --> 00:04:01,080
Captain William Barber kept

99
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:03,026
directing the fight from a stretcher.

100
00:04:03,194 --> 00:04:05,371
Had the pass fallen, the two regiments at

101
00:04:05,391 --> 00:04:08,532
Yudam-ni would have lost their way home.

102
00:04:08,762 --> 00:04:11,463
Most of the company was killed or wounded,

103
00:04:11,483 --> 00:04:14,399
but Fox Company never gave up the pass.

104
00:04:14,488 --> 00:04:16,619
Captain Barber was later awarded the Medal

105
00:04:16,639 --> 00:04:19,995
of Honor, America's highest decoration.

106
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:22,366
On December 1st, the two Marine

107
00:04:22,386 --> 00:04:24,369
regiments at Yudam-ni began

108
00:04:24,389 --> 00:04:26,533
their breakout toward Hagaru-ri.

109
00:04:26,781 --> 00:04:28,927
After three days of fighting through snow

110
00:04:28,947 --> 00:04:32,148
along the ridgelines, they reached Hagaru-ri.

111
00:04:32,218 --> 00:04:34,144
They loaded their wounded onto trucks

112
00:04:34,164 --> 00:04:36,545
and brought out their dead as well.

113
00:04:36,770 --> 00:04:38,881
At Hagaru-ri, there was the airstrip

114
00:04:38,901 --> 00:04:41,157
the Marines had prepared in advance.

115
00:04:41,263 --> 00:04:44,021
Transports shuttled across the frozen runway,

116
00:04:44,041 --> 00:04:46,725
flying out more than 4,000 wounded.

117
00:04:46,968 --> 00:04:48,013
There was even an offer to

118
00:04:48,033 --> 00:04:49,629
fly the whole division out.

119
00:04:49,717 --> 00:04:52,304
But Smith refused. They would walk out,

120
00:04:52,324 --> 00:04:54,914
and they would not abandon their equipment.

121
00:04:55,129 --> 00:04:56,491
It was then that Smith is

122
00:04:56,511 --> 00:04:58,289
said to have told reporters:

123
00:04:58,309 --> 00:05:00,440
"We are not retreating. We are merely

124
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:02,274
attacking in another direction."

125
00:05:02,506 --> 00:05:04,513
On December 6th, the Marines

126
00:05:04,533 --> 00:05:06,930
left Hagaru-ri for Koto-ri.

127
00:05:06,959 --> 00:05:09,318
All night, Chinese fire poured down from

128
00:05:09,338 --> 00:05:11,658
the ridges on both sides of the road.

129
00:05:11,894 --> 00:05:13,908
From Hagaru-ri to Koto-ri

130
00:05:13,928 --> 00:05:16,241
was about 18 kilometers.

131
00:05:16,361 --> 00:05:18,355
Getting thousands of vehicles and men

132
00:05:18,375 --> 00:05:20,776
through took more than a full day.

133
00:05:20,995 --> 00:05:23,752
Overhead, Marine Corsair fighter-bombers

134
00:05:23,772 --> 00:05:26,187
hammered the ridgelines without pause.

135
00:05:26,285 --> 00:05:27,984
When the troops below called,

136
00:05:28,004 --> 00:05:31,712
they arrived within minutes and dropped napalm.

137
00:05:31,951 --> 00:05:34,417
Then, south of Koto-ri at Funchilin

138
00:05:34,437 --> 00:05:37,023
Pass, came the worst news of all.

139
00:05:37,044 --> 00:05:38,823
The Chinese had blown the bridge

140
00:05:38,843 --> 00:05:41,276
over the gorge. The road was cut.

141
00:05:41,496 --> 00:05:42,931
The gap was about five meters,

142
00:05:42,951 --> 00:05:44,285
above a sheer drop.

143
00:05:44,356 --> 00:05:46,532
Without a bridge, no truck, gun,

144
00:05:46,552 --> 00:05:49,055
or wounded man could get across.

145
00:05:49,291 --> 00:05:50,635
The Marines' answer was to

146
00:05:50,655 --> 00:05:52,637
drop a bridge from the sky.

147
00:05:52,707 --> 00:05:55,554
On December 7th, eight C-119

148
00:05:55,574 --> 00:05:58,662
transports parachuted 1.3-ton steel

149
00:05:58,682 --> 00:06:01,145
bridge sections onto Koto-ri.

150
00:06:01,370 --> 00:06:03,027
One was smashed on landing,

151
00:06:03,047 --> 00:06:05,702
and one drifted toward Chinese lines,

152
00:06:05,723 --> 00:06:06,841
but the four sections they

153
00:06:06,861 --> 00:06:08,815
needed came down intact.

154
00:06:09,034 --> 00:06:11,303
On December 9th, engineers laid the

155
00:06:11,323 --> 00:06:13,977
bridge across the gorge in brutal cold.

156
00:06:14,146 --> 00:06:16,323
At last, the Marines' long

157
00:06:16,343 --> 00:06:18,664
column began to cross the chasm.

158
00:06:18,919 --> 00:06:22,234
Meanwhile, the 1st Battalion, 1st Marines,

159
00:06:22,254 --> 00:06:23,771
fought through a blizzard to

160
00:06:23,791 --> 00:06:26,797
seize Hill 1081 overlooking the bridge.

161
00:06:26,966 --> 00:06:28,267
Its job was to protect

162
00:06:28,287 --> 00:06:30,186
the engineers from above.

163
00:06:30,423 --> 00:06:31,815
Even on the way down the pass,

164
00:06:31,835 --> 00:06:34,079
they did not leave their dead behind.

165
00:06:34,206 --> 00:06:36,201
They carried their frozen comrades on

166
00:06:36,221 --> 00:06:38,798
trucks and on guns as they marched.

167
00:06:39,028 --> 00:06:41,258
On December 11th, the last units

168
00:06:41,278 --> 00:06:43,227
reached the plain of Hamhung.

169
00:06:43,350 --> 00:06:45,151
Two weeks after being surrounded,

170
00:06:45,171 --> 00:06:48,038
the 1st Marine Division had reached the sea.

171
00:06:48,286 --> 00:06:50,224
How did a surrounded division break through

172
00:06:50,244 --> 00:06:52,920
four times its number, and the cold?

173
00:06:52,969 --> 00:06:55,116
Let's break it down into four factors.

174
00:06:55,349 --> 00:06:57,862
First, Smith's slow advance.

175
00:06:58,102 --> 00:07:01,152
Despite pressure from above, he built up supplies,

176
00:07:01,172 --> 00:07:03,815
cut an airstrip, and held the road.

177
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:06,538
Without that preparation, Hagaru-ri could

178
00:07:06,558 --> 00:07:09,086
never have become a base for rescue.

179
00:07:09,319 --> 00:07:11,947
Second, support from the air.

180
00:07:12,177 --> 00:07:14,019
Close air support, supply drops,

181
00:07:14,039 --> 00:07:15,794
and casualty airlifts.

182
00:07:15,849 --> 00:07:17,058
And a bridge dropped from

183
00:07:17,078 --> 00:07:18,940
the sky saved the division.

184
00:07:19,188 --> 00:07:21,726
Third, cohesion and discipline.

185
00:07:21,968 --> 00:07:24,276
The units held formation to the end

186
00:07:24,296 --> 00:07:27,167
and never abandoned their comrades.

187
00:07:27,224 --> 00:07:29,002
That discipline kept the column from

188
00:07:29,022 --> 00:07:31,713
breaking, even in the grip of fear.

189
00:07:31,957 --> 00:07:33,659
Fourth, the cold hit the

190
00:07:33,679 --> 00:07:35,158
Chinese even harder.

191
00:07:35,415 --> 00:07:38,094
Even by Chinese count, combat casualties

192
00:07:38,114 --> 00:07:41,293
exceeded 21,000, with over 30,000 more

193
00:07:41,313 --> 00:07:43,842
lost to frostbite and other causes.

194
00:07:44,010 --> 00:07:46,278
The 1st Marine Division also lost more

195
00:07:46,298 --> 00:07:49,647
than 7,000 men to frostbite and illness,

196
00:07:49,667 --> 00:07:52,422
on top of its battle casualties.

197
00:07:52,667 --> 00:07:55,045
The Chinese 9th Army Group was unable to

198
00:07:55,065 --> 00:07:57,566
fight for nearly three months afterward.

199
00:07:57,797 --> 00:08:00,113
At Hungnam, the port the Marines had

200
00:08:00,133 --> 00:08:03,209
fought to reach, another miracle began.

201
00:08:03,315 --> 00:08:08,350
By December 24th, 105,000 troops and 98,000

202
00:08:08,370 --> 00:08:11,466
refugees had been taken aboard ships.

203
00:08:11,678 --> 00:08:13,602
The freighter SS Meredith Victory

204
00:08:13,622 --> 00:08:17,233
alone carried about 14,000 refugees.

205
00:08:17,356 --> 00:08:19,419
It would later be recognized as the

206
00:08:19,439 --> 00:08:22,250
greatest rescue ever made by a single ship.

207
00:08:22,503 --> 00:08:24,133
After the last ship sailed,

208
00:08:24,153 --> 00:08:26,365
the docks at Hungnam were blown up.

209
00:08:26,615 --> 00:08:28,274
Those who came back from Chosin

210
00:08:28,294 --> 00:08:31,005
called themselves 'The Chosin Few.'

211
00:08:31,085 --> 00:08:32,886
Across America, memorials

212
00:08:32,906 --> 00:08:35,287
to them still stand today.

213
00:08:35,533 --> 00:08:37,534
And among the refugees they carried

214
00:08:37,554 --> 00:08:40,186
to safety were the parents and grandparents

215
00:08:40,206 --> 00:08:42,434
of many Koreans living today.

216
00:08:42,825 --> 00:08:45,926
A frozen gorge, and a single road.

217
00:08:46,044 --> 00:08:47,799
Those who walked it to the end were the

218
00:08:47,819 --> 00:08:50,679
ones who never left a comrade behind.

219
00:08:50,907 --> 00:08:52,589
If this story meant something to you,

220
00:08:52,609 --> 00:08:55,081
please subscribe and like the video.

221
00:08:55,204 --> 00:08:56,368
We'll continue to bring the

222
00:08:56,388 --> 00:08:58,656
forgotten records of war to light.
